WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.033
Exception handling.

2
00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:05.203
Timeouts, rate limits, and malformed JSON.

3
00:00:05.203 --> 00:00:09.167
Real AI systems spend most of their code handling failure.

4
00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:13.000
The full shape is try, except, else, finally.

5
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:17.767
Catch only what you can actually handle, and name the specific exception class.

6
00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:20.700
A bare except swallows bugs you wanted to see.

7
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:22.900
Here's the flow.

8
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:24.833
The risky call goes in try.

9
00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:27.167
If it raises, we land in except.

10
00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:29.867
If nothing goes wrong, else runs instead.

11
00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:32.900
And finally catches both paths, no matter what.

12
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.000
Two habits make failures readable.

13
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.719
Custom exception classes make failure modes explicit.

14
00:00:39.719 --> 00:00:43.267
A timeout and a bad payload deserve different handling.

15
00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:48.600
And raise from keeps the causal chain, so the traceback shows the original error underneath.

16
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.767
The retry waits longer each time.

17
00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:56.396
Two seconds after the first failure, then four seconds after the second.

18
00:00:56.396 --> 00:00:58.500
On the third, we give up and raise.

19
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.200
Here's the pattern in code.

20
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.600
We try the flaky call inside a loop.

21
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.567
We catch only the timeout error.

22
00:01:05.867 --> 00:01:09.800
On the last attempt, we raise our own error, chained from the original.

23
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.067
Otherwise we sleep, with the wait growing each time.

24
00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:16.367
Here's why it matters in a live agent.

25
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:20.633
When the space-weather service is unreachable, the tool wraps this pattern.

26
00:01:20.933 --> 00:01:27.733
It returns a calm unavailable message for the model, instead of an unhandled exception killing the whole run.

27
00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:32.600
And thanks to raise from, the trace shows the original timeout as the root cause.

28
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:34.571
Let's recap.

29
00:01:34.571 --> 00:01:37.333
Try, except, else, finally gives two paths.

30
00:01:37.633 --> 00:01:39.567
Catch specific exceptions only.

31
00:01:39.867 --> 00:01:41.667
Use custom exception classes.

32
00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:44.508
And raise from keeps the original cause.

33
00:01:44.508 --> 00:01:46.033
Next up: multithreading.
